> By the way, eu1.alt.orgdoes not respond anymore...

Yep, a few kinks to still work out. The slowness is anytime during
file reads or writes:

1) dgamelaunch logins. Not sure how to address this one if I want
people to be able to create or change logins from other server. I
guess I could force new accounts and changes through the master and
just have eu1 work off of a sync'd copy. That would let it work
locally. The other option would be to move the login db to a queryable
DB instead of a flat file DB to keep file transfer down.

2) Game saves/loads. No real way around this one if we want people to
be able to save on one and resume on another. Having it all sync on
one filesystem also reduces the weird problems of resolving 2
simultaneous logins from one account on both servers. Luckily this is
not a common thing since only twice per session (one save, one load).

3) Level changes. Lots of fs writes on these. I guess I could make
those local instead of over network. Have to look into that.

4) Game watching. Anything involving the inprogress ttyrec files
across the ocean is slow. Main problem is that nfs/cifs want to buffer
file reads/writes so that we're not sending a new back-and-forth for
every 10 bytes of data. The ttyrec file watching likes to be byte-by-
byte fast. For now, things basically update every 30 seconds or so in
a normal speed game, which isn't so much like watching real time. I
can probably crank this down a good bit by changing buffering settings
for that specific mount, although I'm not sure it will ever be turn-by-
turn fast with this method. The proper way to do this is probably to
change dgamelaunch to just re-stream the ttyrec file from a remote tty
player, which is work I haven't done yet.

Things I've worked out some:

5) Static nethack level files and and the binary itself can be local,
and once I changed that, things sped up a lot.

6) The performance doesn't substantially change whether I'm using
nfsv3, nfsv4, or cifs as the network file mount. This is just a
straight matter of latency when having to do a quick operation of a
few megabytes (including some back and forth with file locks) across
the Atlantic Ocean. It really only takes a second to a few seconds,
but that seems like an eternity on a tty.

Anyway, this is a project that still needs some work, and probably
some changes to dgamelaunch, but is something I'll look at again
sometime. (Especially if I can get some friendly at Amazon to spot me
a discounted EC2 instance for it? ;)

